## Wikipedia contributors — CC BY-SA 4.0

The descriptive H1–H9 taxonomy, its caution against binary detection, and the
separation between surface indicators and evidence problems in
`skills/tool/q-tool-humanizer/references/patterns-en.md` and `patterns-es.md`
adapt these pages:

- [Wikipedia:Signs of AI writing, revision 1369279283](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1369279283&title=Wikipedia%3ASigns_of_AI_writing), 14 August 2026.
- [Wikipedia:WikiProject AI Cleanup, revision 1369089165](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?oldid=1369089165&title=Wikipedia%3AWikiProject_AI_Cleanup), 13 August 2026.

Authors are the Wikipedia contributors identified in each page history. These
adapted portions are licensed under the [Creative Commons
Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International license](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/).
Quasar condensed and generalized the field guide, assigned stable H IDs,
removed Wikipedia-only policy and markup procedures, added bilingual and
owner-routing rules, and replaced source examples with original examples. No
endorsement by Wikipedia or its contributors is implied. Original Quasar
material outside those adapted portions remains under the Quasar MIT license.

## William Strunk Jr. — public-domain principles

The C1–C9 taxonomy in
`skills/tool/q-tool-humanizer/references/clarity-en.md` and `clarity-es.md` is
conceptually informed by composition principles in William Strunk Jr.'s early
*The Elements of Style*. The source skill identifies the 1918 text; [Project
Gutenberg ebook 37134](https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/37134) provides a 1920
edition and marks it public domain in the United States. Quasar incorporates no
verbatim Strunk prose, examples, usage chapter, manuscript-formatting chapter,
or historically dated word list. It distills selected principles through
original modern examples and independently adapts them for Spanish.
